  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: High pressure continues to spread west \nas the week progresses. Thunderstorm potential still exists today \nalong the Utah border in both Elko and White Pine Counties. The \nhigh pressure system is expected to expand far enough west by \nThursday to keep moisture out of the forecast area. Smoke from \nboth local and Pacific Northwest wildfires is also expected to \nhave local impacts during this time, particularly in northern \nNevada. A very smoky morning was observed in Elko today and \nseveral more are expected to follow. Daytime southerly to \nsouthwest flow is expected to help clear smoke during the days, \nhowever calm or northerly flow during the nights will allow smoke \nto increase for the late night to early morning hours. Smoke \nconcentrations are expected to be high enough to cause issues for \nsensitive groups, especially during early morning when \nconcentrations will be highest. The final focus of this forecast \nwill be the heat. Temperatures are expected to climb as the high \npressure system establishes itself and widespread Major HeatRisk \nlevels are expected by Friday and especially Saturday. Widespread \nhigh temperatures of 100 to 105 degrees are expected for Saturday,\nwhich is 10 to 15 degrees above normal for this time of year. Low\ntemperatures will be in the 60s, somewhat limiting overnight \nrecoveries. While the heat is expected to be at its worst on \nSaturday, long term outlooks indicate it\u2019s not leaving any time \nsoon, with intense heat favored through mid to late next week.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence for smoke \nimpacts through at least Thursday. High confidence in extreme \nheat, particularly for this weekend. NBM was modified for POP, \nPotThunder and Wx for this afternoon and evening to relay the 10 \nto 20% chance of thunderstorm activity across eastern NV.",
